2186176685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176741 #edb/ref 1061 33947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4a2022f-0bfe-53a7-a3a3-924b5ed19a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176751 #edb/ref 1061 34228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77279341-1e22-5bb1-bd2c-8981a3235685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176753 #edb/ref 1061 34238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5dae560-1167-5534-8639-30d7fa5c56b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176847 #edb/ref 1061 38212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f44e66b4-ae69-5a8a-b61a-38a70551aa2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177003 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment**, respectively.\n\nIt's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm E[X^3]$ is the **third moment.**\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)$ is the first moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ is the second moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^3] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^3 \\cdot f(x)$ is the third moment\n- $\\,\\cdots$\n\nIn general, the $n$th moment is given by\n\n$$\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^n] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^n \\cdot f(x).$$\n\nThe concept of a moment will become important later. In particular, we'll soon see how $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ can be used to compute the **variance** of a random variable.\n\nFinally, note that $\\textrm E[X], \\textrm E[X^2], \\textrm E[X^3],$ etc. are sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moment.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177003 #edb/ref 1065 5499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177003 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0f377288-40af-50ed-b77c-90edf07bb005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177003 #edb/ref 1067 "The Moments of a Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177004 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for a discrete random variable $X$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X,$ denoted $\\textrm E[X],$ is given by\n\n$$\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x),$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability mass function of $X.$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the expected value of the random variable $X^2$ as\n\n$$\\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x).$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIn this case, $\\textrm E[X^2]$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\ & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+9\\cdot 0.25 \\\\ & =4.5.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing $\\textrm E[X^2],$ it's important to remember that we still evaluate the probability mass function as $f(x),$ *not* $f(x^2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177004 #edb/ref 1065 5503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "544551ff-1bd9-5834-8bed-084fe6b81425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute $\\text{E}[(2Y-8)^{2}]$ if $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=4$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26890fb4-88b9-5839-a1c9-c4e1304b8fae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367061 #edb/ref 1074 "Expanding out the square inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(2Y-8)^{2}] & =\\text{E}[4{Y}^{2}-32Y+64] \\\\  & =4\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-32\\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[64] \\\\  & =4\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-32\\text{E}[Y]+64 \\\\  & =4(4)-32(1)+64 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367062 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73f57246-158a-5d2f-9ec2-9042aba97d56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367063 #edb/ref 1080 "64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367063 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367063 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "731bcb55-0ca1-5b0a-a179-b008b1fd9f62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367064 #edb/ref 1080 "46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e75ac44-18b0-5c6a-b46e-28e02a663c83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367065 #edb/ref 1080 "40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367065 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367065 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87786e89-7293-55e5-a71c-952724c76ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367066 #edb/ref 1080 "50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3f4f66a-0854-52ec-b00d-f15c3faf1d00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367067 #edb/ref 1080 "48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367067 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367067 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "122cf90f-5bbe-5112-a195-784d781c642d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1071 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c68c663-d77f-5a12-85f4-75aca5eeb431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367212 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{7}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+25\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+49\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367213 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0494f5c6-4107-563c-a585-1687bd2827c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367214 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{21}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367214 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367214 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa850acb-3fde-5f4a-bfa9-139a31b031df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367215 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{21}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11016637-5996-50ad-9a83-a0f0f6ad3935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367216 #edb/ref 1080 "21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367216 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367216 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2a47e01-a255-5a0a-8a9c-bb533a113fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367217 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{41}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367217 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367217 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f58c812-e9a9-5af9-b4f3-9e914ee43ec5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367218 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{21}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367218 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367218 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "583435ac-e60d-5d39-9b12-410d744b6169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1071 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b92528f1-22be-5c5f-a1fc-bc9c1f101a38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367582 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{4}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of ${X}^{4}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{4}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{4}\\cdot 0.35+{3}^{4}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+16\\cdot 0.35+81\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.25+5.6+32.4 \\\\  & =38.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367583 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186